From: chasey-dev <ellenyoung0912@gmail.com>
Subject: [mt_wifi7] 067 别让芯片在 patch 跳转时把自己复位掉

Every boot so far loses the chip about 115 ms after PATCH_FINISH_REQ, and
until now that was read as the patch crashing.  It is a reset, and the chip
is the one doing it: the PCI COMMAND word reads back zero, BAR0 still holds
0x20000000, and the block returns with every WFDMA ring register cleared -
base, size and both indices - which is why the recovery has to reprogram all
of them.  The reset unit has a register for exactly this, FLR_CTL at
0x1f8620, and on a freshly booted chip it reads 0x00030001: FLR_EN set, a
three cycle reset.

Clearing FLR_EN before PATCH_FINISH_REQ keeps the function from resetting
itself, so the rings the driver programmed stay valid across the jump and
the RAM code can be downloaded into the patch that has just started - which
is the order the vendor driver uses and the one that has never survived
here.  The bit is put back once the semaphore exchange is over so a later
real reset still works, and diag_hold_flr=0 restores the old behaviour.

Signed-off-by: chasey-dev <ellenyoung0912@gmail.com>
---
---
--- a/wlan_hwifi/mcu/mcu_wm.c
+++ b/wlan_hwifi/mcu/mcu_wm.c
@@ -551,6 +551,42 @@
 #define DIAG_SWDEF_MODE_ADDR	0x009143C
 
 /*
+ * 067: the endpoint does not just go quiet after PATCH_FINISH_REQ, it is
+ * reset - the PCI COMMAND word reads zero, BAR0 keeps its address, and the
+ * block comes back 115 ms later with every WFDMA ring register at zero.  The
+ * chip owns an FLR controller in its reset unit and it is armed: FLR_CTL
+ * reads 0x00030001, FLR_EN set with a three cycle reset.  Clear that one bit
+ * across the jump and the function cannot reset itself, so the rings the
+ * driver programmed stay valid and the RAM code can go into the patch that
+ * has just started - which is the order the vendor flow needs and the one
+ * that has never survived here.
+ */
+static bool diag_hold_flr = true;
+module_param(diag_hold_flr, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(diag_hold_flr,
+		 "clear FLR_EN across PATCH_FINISH_REQ so the endpoint survives the jump");
+
+#define DIAG_FLR_CTL_ADDR	0x001F8620
+#define DIAG_FLR_CTL_EN		BIT(0)
+
+static void
+mcu_diag_flr_ctl(struct mtk_hw_dev *dev, bool hold)
+{
+	struct mtk_bus_trans *trans = dev->bus_trans;
+	u32 before, after;
+
+	before = bus_read(trans, DIAG_FLR_CTL_ADDR);
+	after = hold ? (before & ~DIAG_FLR_CTL_EN) :
+			(before | DIAG_FLR_CTL_EN);
+	if (after != before)
+		bus_write(trans, DIAG_FLR_CTL_ADDR, after);
+
+	dev_err(dev->dev, "diag: flr %s 0x%08x -> 0x%08x (now 0x%08x)\n",
+		hold ? "hold" : "release", before, after,
+		bus_read(trans, DIAG_FLR_CTL_ADDR));
+}
+
+/*
  * 047 switches.  The chip survives a download that never jumps: with
  * diag_skip_start=1 the config space still reads command=0x0006 and
  * BAR0=0x20000000 afterwards, so the scatter itself is harmless and the
@@ -780,6 +816,9 @@
 		if (ret)
 			dev_err(dev->dev, "diag: early ram failed %d\n", ret);
 	} else if (!diag_late_patch_finish) {
+		if (diag_hold_flr)
+			mcu_diag_flr_ctl(dev, true);
+
 		ret = mcu_wm_start_patch(dev);
 		if (ret)
 			dev_err(dev->dev, "Failed to start patch\n");
@@ -814,6 +853,9 @@
 		mcu_diag_chip(dev, "post-rel");
 	}
 
+	if (diag_hold_flr)
+		mcu_diag_flr_ctl(dev, false);
+
 	if (diag_own_retry) {
 		int own = mtk_hw_set_driver_own(dev, dev->bus_trans, true);
 
